- op: create  # f4ce
  at: '2026-08-25T08:37:47Z'  # f4ce
  by: agent  # f4ce
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f4ce
  clock: 1  # f4ce
  nonce: f4ce  # f4ce
  data:  # f4ce
    kind: invariant  # f4ce
    title: "document.location / document.referrer are attacker-influenceable, so labelling them host_secret gets the DIRECTION of the trust relationship wrong (one catalogue row spans both ends of the axis)"  # f4ce
    status: violated  # f4ce
    priority: 3  # f4ce
    parent:  # f4ce
    tags:  # f4ce
    - taint  # f4ce
    - io_boundary  # f4ce
    isbefore: []  # f4ce
    duplicate_of: []  # f4ce
    not_duplicate_of: []  # f4ce
    pr_ref:  # f4ce
    description: "FOUND during INV-tutar's env_read census, 2026-08-25, and deliberately NOT\nfolded into that split: it is a different defect on a different axis.\n\nINV-tutar separated a CONFIGURATION read from a DESCRIPTION read -- both are\nthings the program reads ABOUT its environment, and the question was how\nsensitive the value is. This is not that. document.location and\ndocument.referrer are values an ATTACKER can set, which makes a flow out of\nthem the untrusted-input shape (attacker data reaching a sink), not the\nsecret-exfiltration shape (secret data reaching an egress). Labelling them\nhost_secret gets the DIRECTION of the trust relationship wrong, not just its\nmagnitude.\n\nTHE THIRD ROW-MATE IS GENUINELY DIFFERENT AGAIN. document.cookie holds session\ntokens; host_secret is right for it, and it should stay where it is. So one\ncatalogue row currently spans both ends of the trust axis.\n\nCURRENT STATE, verified at tip after PR #526:\n    javascript document.cookie   -> env_read -> host_secret   (correct)\n    javascript document.location -> env_read -> host_secret   (wrong direction)\n    javascript document.referrer -> env_read -> host_secret   (wrong direction)\n\nWHAT THE FIX PROBABLY IS, unbuilt: move location/referrer to net_recv, which\nalready derives untrusted_input, and split the row so cookie keeps its own.\nWhether net_recv is the right boundary for a DOM read is the open question --\nnothing was received over the network at that call site, the value merely\noriginated there -- so this may want the same treatment INV-tutar got rather\nthan a reuse. Do not assume; run the four leakage tests on\n(net_recv, untrusted_input) against a DOM read before moving anything.\n\nSCOPE NOT MEASURED. Three rows, javascript/typescript only. Whether it shows up\nin a real precision number depends on how often browser code in the corpus\nreads document.referrer; that was not counted."  # f4ce
    fields:  # f4ce
      statement: "A taint source label must name what the value IS. document.location and document.referrer carry ATTACKER-INFLUENCEABLE data, so they are untrusted_input sources; only document.cookie is credential material."  # f4ce
      root_cause: "io_primitives/javascript.yaml files document.cookie, document.location and document.referrer under one env_read row with the note \"Browser document globals (cookie/referrer leak fingerprintable data)\". AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP derives host_secret from env_read, so all three become SECRET sources. Two of the three are the opposite end of the trust axis: an attacker controls the referrer and can control the URL, so a flow FROM them is untrusted INPUT reaching a sink, not a secret escaping."  # f4ce
- op: update  # 6769
  at: '2026-09-29T05:12:00Z'  # 6769
  by: agent  # 6769
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 6769
  clock: 2  # 6769
  nonce: '6769'  # 6769
  set:  # 6769
    title: "document.location / document.referrer are attacker-influenceable but derive no untrusted_input source (they stay host_secret: a URL may carry a credential) -- blocked on a code/DOM-injection sink (WI-nokab)"  # 6769
    fields:  # 6769
      statement: "A browser value an attacker can set is an untrusted_input source. document.location and document.referrer are attacker-influenceable AND may carry a credential (a token in the URL or leaked referrer), so they are host_secret (env_read, per the INV-nular ruling that env_read means may-carry-a-credential) AND must also be untrusted_input."  # 6769
      root_cause: "javascript.yaml rows them under env_read only; the only way to derive a second label is a second boundary (INV-tutar), and no boundary means 'attacker-chosen content that crossed earlier' (net_recv means a receive at the call site). Payoff is blocked: taint ships no code/DOM-injection sink (WI-nokab)."  # 6769
- op: discuss  # 9f2e
  at: '2026-09-29T05:12:00Z'  # 9f2e
  by: agent  # 9f2e
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9f2e
  clock: 3  # 9f2e
  nonce: 9f2e  # 9f2e
  message: "RE-SCOPED 2026-09-29 (owner decision: re-scope, not the prescribed move). The item's premise was half wrong and its prescribed fix would have been a regression.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG. The statement said \"only document.cookie is credential material\". But the owner ruling on env_read (INV-nular, 2026-09-06, recorded on elixir.yaml's Application.get_env row) is that env_read means MAY CARRY A CREDENTIAL, not \"reads the environment\" -- the definition that kept argv in env_read in every catalogue although an attacker can set argv too. A page URL can carry an OAuth token (the implicit-flow #access_token fragment) or a password-reset token, and document.referrer leaks the previous page's URL with whatever it carried (why Referrer-Policy exists). The env-read census that found this item itself filed all three as \"credential-bearing browser state\" (docs/surveys/env-read-boundary-census.md, family E). Moving location / referrer to net_recv would have DROPPED host_secret sources -- the false-all-clear direction -- and made io-boundaries report a network receive on every page that reads its own URL, although nothing is received at that call site (net_recv's registry meaning).\n\nWHAT STAYS TRUE. Both values are ALSO attacker-influenceable, and today they derive no untrusted_input source. That is this item now. It is BLOCKED: the payoff of an untrusted_input label here is DOM-XSS / code injection, and taint ships no such sink (WI-nokab, which blocks this item). Measured on dev: document.location -> fetch IS found as host_secret -> network (the token-leak case works); bare eval(r) emits no edge; innerHTML assignment emits none; code_execution is not a valid zone.\n\nPARITY FIX, done separately under this item: window.location was host_info_read (host_description) while document.location is env_read -- the same URL under two boundaries. Moved to env_read (PR to follow; the A/B is on its commit).\n\nWHEN UNBLOCKED: the owner-sanctioned mechanism for a second label is a BOUNDARY, not a per-row label (INV-tutar: \"the split is of the boundary, not the label\"), and net_recv fails the mechanism test -- so this wants a new inbound boundary deriving untrusted_input, declared alongside env_read on these rows."  # 9f2e
- op: discuss  # c08e
  at: '2026-09-29T06:33:27Z'  # c08e
  by: agent  # c08e
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c08e
  clock: 4  # c08e
  nonce: c08e  # c08e
  message: "PARITY PART DONE in PR #1292 (dev 97ff6e24ef + f12ca53d8e + 24735aac13): window.location moved host_info_read -> env_read beside document.location; document's row note now records why it stays env_read. Re-verified on merged dev: const u = window.location.href; fetch(url + u) -> host_secret -> network VIOLATED 1 flow (was CONFIRMED 0, host_info_read). A/B on 8 JS/TS repos (~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/dadu_09292026/RESULT.md): 8 verdicts inconclusive -> violated, flows +47 / -15 where all 15 '-' are the same window.location flows relabelled host_description -> host_secret. Read back: growthbook oauth/callback.tsx POSTs window.location.search/hash (the OAuth code) with fetch -- the credential-in-URL case, previously invisible to host_secret claims. This item stays VIOLATED for its re-scoped statement (no untrusted_input source), blocked on WI-nokab."  # c08e
- op: discuss  # 4f02
  at: '2026-09-29T13:26:25Z'  # 4f02
  by: agent  # 4f02
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4f02
  clock: 5  # 4f02
  nonce: 4f02  # 4f02
  message: "UNBLOCKED 2026-09-29: WI-nokab shipped code_execution and dom_injection sinks (PR #1301, ADR-0060). On dev, document.referrer -> eval / window.eval is a host_secret -> code_execution violation and -> document.write a host_secret -> dom_injection violation today, under the owner's 09-29 ruling that location/referrer stay env_read. What remains on this item is unchanged and still the owner's: whether the page URL/referrer ALSO carries an attacker-input label (untrusted_input). innerHTML assignment and the other unreached shapes are WI-bonah."  # 4f02
- op: discuss  # 3ff8
  at: '2026-09-29T20:33:27Z'  # 3ff8
  by: agent  # 3ff8
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 3ff8
  clock: 6  # 3ff8
  nonce: 3ff8  # 3ff8
  message: "INVESTIGATED 2026-09-29 on dev 596b845156, for the owner's question. document.location.hash -> eval under untrusted_input -> code_execution: inconclusive rc 2, and the same under dom_injection with document.write. Under host_secret both read violated. So today the canonical DOM-XSS claim can never read violated when the flow starts at the page URL. It reads inconclusive only because the eval/document.write call trips the unclassified-module gate, filed as INV-dudal. Fixing INV-dudal before this item would turn that into a false confirmed. Recommendation and mechanism options are with the owner."  # 3ff8
- op: discuss  # 239a
  at: '2026-09-29T20:39:20Z'  # 239a
  by: agent  # 239a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 239a
  clock: 7  # 239a
  nonce: 239a  # 239a
  message: "OWNER DECISION 09-29: YES -- the page URL/referrer ALSO carry an attacker-input label, and keep host_secret. Mechanism (agent recommendation, approved): a new inbound boundary navigation_read deriving untrusted_input, declared SIMULTANEOUS with env_read on document.location / window.location / document.referrer, plus the unrowed document.URL / documentURI / baseURI / window.name. Not net_recv (nothing is received at the call site), not a shipped taint_sources file (sources merge on (module,name,kind): it would DISPLACE the host_secret source). Built-in tier (DOM standard). Lands before INV-dudal."  # 239a
